      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To further test the validity of the Biobehavioral Family Model (BBFM), a biopsychosocial approach to explaining the effects of family processes on illness, by testing the model with adolescents with asthma involved in child protective services (CPS), a sample for whom the model's constructs are highly relevant."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Data are from the National Survey of Child and Adolescent Well-Being, CPS sample (n\u2009=\u20095,501). Adolescents with asthma (n\u2009=\u2009101, ages 11-15 years) self-reported their negative family emotional climate (NFEC; caregiver psychological aggression and caregiver relationship quality), caregiver-adolescent relational security, and depressive symptoms. Caregivers rated adolescent health quality. Models were tested using path analyses and bootstrapping."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Path analyses and bootstrapping results demonstrate support for the BBFM in explaining health quality for this sample."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Applying the BBFM to families involved with CPS demonstrates pathways by which family processes affect health quality of adolescents with asthma, underscoring the need for biopsychosocial assessments and services."
        }
      ]},
